/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/pcep.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("pcep", [["pc1",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(7 // 2, 7 % 2, -7 // 2)",["3 1 -3","3.5 1 -3.5","3 -1 -4","3 1 -4"],3,"// is floor division, so 7 // 2 is 3 and -7 // 2 rounds down to -4, not toward zero (-3). 7 % 2 is 1. Neither operator returns a float for int operands.","Objective 1.4 Operators"],["pc2",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(2 ** 3 ** 2)",["512","64","36","12"],0,"The ** operator is right-associative, so 2 ** 3 ** 2 is 2 ** (3 ** 2) = 2 ** 9 = 512. Reading it left to right gives the tempting 64.","Objective 1.4 Operators"],["pc3",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(0o17 + 0x10 + 0b11)",["44","40","34","28"],2,"0o17 is octal 15, 0x10 is hex 16 and 0b11 is binary 3, so the sum is 34. Literals in different bases are all just ints and add normally.","Objective 1.3 Literals"],["pc4",0,1,"A student expects this code to print 13. What does it actually print?\nx = 5\nx *= 2 + 3\nprint(x)",["13","25","10","28"],1,"A compound assignment evaluates the whole right side first: x *= 2 + 3 means x = x * (2 + 3) = 25. The 13 answer wrongly applies x * 2 before adding 3.","Objective 1.4 Operators"],["pc5",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(1 / 2 * 4)",["2","0.125","0","2.0"],3,"/ always returns a float, and * and / have equal priority evaluated left to right: (1 / 2) * 4 = 2.0. The int 2 would need //.","Objective 1.4 Operators"],["pc6",0,1,"A script prints a date-like string. What is the exact output?\nprint(1, 2, 3, sep=\"-\", end=\"!\")",["1 2 3!","1-2-3-!","-1-2-3!","1-2-3!"],3,"sep is placed only between arguments and end replaces the final newline, so the output is 1-2-3!. sep is not added after the last argument.","Objective 1.5 Console I/O"],["pc7",0,1,"A user types 5 and presses Enter when this program runs. What is printed?\nx = input()\nprint(x * 2)",["10","55","5 5","A TypeError"],1,"input() always returns a string, so x is '5' and '5' * 2 repeats it to '55'. To get 10 the value must first be converted with int().","Objective 1.5 Console I/O"],["pc8",0,1,"A developer is naming variables for a new script. Which name is not a legal Python identifier?",["_total2","total_2nd","Total","2nd_total"],3,"Identifiers cannot start with a digit, so 2nd_total is a syntax error. Leading underscores, digits after the first character and capital letters are all legal.","Objective 1.3 Variables"],["pc9",0,1,"Which of these words is a reserved Python keyword that cannot be used as a variable name?",["print","input","null","None"],3,"None is a keyword. print and input are built-in functions (they can be shadowed, though you should not), and null has no meaning in Python.","Objective 1.2 Keywords"],["pc10",0,1,"Which statement correctly describes interpreting compared with compiling, as the terms are used in PCEP?",["An interpreter translates and runs source code each time it is run","An interpreter produces a stand-alone machine-code file once","A compiler checks only indentation and leaves syntax to run time","Interpreted languages cannot report syntax errors at all"],0,"An interpreter reads and executes the source each run, while a compiler translates the whole program once into a machine-code file that runs without the source. The stand-alone executable describes compilation, not interpretation.","Objective 1.1 Fundamental terms"],["pc11",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(bool(0.0), bool(\"0\"))",["False False","False True","True True","True False"],1,"Zero values and empty strings are False; any non-empty string is True, even '0'. So bool(0.0) is False and bool('0') is True.","Objective 1.4 Type casting"],["pc12",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(int(\"12\") + float(\"3.5\"))",["123.5","15.5","15","A TypeError"],1,"int('12') is 12 and float('3.5') is 3.5; adding an int and a float gives the float 15.5. The strings are converted before the + runs, so there is no concatenation.","Objective 1.4 Type casting"],["pc13",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(6 & 3, 6 | 3, 6 ^ 3)",["1 7 5","2 5 7","2 7 5","3 7 5"],2,"6 is 110 and 3 is 011 in binary. AND gives 010 (2), OR gives 111 (7) and XOR gives 101 (5). XOR keeps only bits set in exactly one operand.","Objective 1.4 Bitwise operators"],["pc14",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(~5, 1 << 3)",["-5 8","4 8","-6 6","-6 8"],3,"~x equals -x - 1, so ~5 is -6. 1 << 3 shifts left three bits, multiplying by 2 ** 3 = 8. ~ is not simple negation, which is why -5 is wrong.","Objective 1.4 Bitwise operators"],["pc15",0,1,"A billing script compares money amounts stored as floats. What does this line print?\nprint(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3)",["False","True","0.3","A ValueError"],0,"0.1 and 0.2 cannot be stored exactly in binary floating point, so their sum is 0.30000000000000004 and the comparison is False. Floats have limited accuracy.","Objective 1.4 Float accuracy"],["pc16",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(10 - 2 * 3 ** 2)",["-8","72","-26","64"],0,"** binds tightest, then *, then -: 3 ** 2 = 9, 2 * 9 = 18, 10 - 18 = -8. 72 comes from wrongly doing 10 - 2 first.","Objective 1.4 Operator priority"],["pc17",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(1e3)",["1000","1e3","0.001","1000.0"],3,"A literal in scientific notation such as 1e3 is always a float, so it prints 1000.0. It is 1 times 10 to the power 3, not 10 to the power -3.","Objective 1.3 Literals"],["pc18",0,2,"What does this code print?\nx = 7\nif x > 5:\n    print(\"A\")\nelif x > 3:\n    print(\"B\")\nelse:\n    print(\"C\")",["B","C","A","A, then B on the next line"],2,"Only the first true branch of an if-elif-else chain runs. x > 5 is true, so A prints and the elif is skipped even though x > 3 is also true.","Objective 2.1 Conditional statements"],["pc19",0,2,"What does this loop print?\nfor i in range(2, 10, 3):\n    print(i, end=\" \")",["2 5 8 11","2 5 8","3 6 9","2 4 6 8"],1,"range(2, 10, 3) starts at 2 and steps by 3 while staying below 10: 2, 5, 8. The stop value is never included, so 11 is not produced.","Objective 2.2 range()"],["pc20",0,2,"What does this loop print?\nfor i in range(5, 0, -2):\n    print(i, end=\" \")",["5 3 1 -1","5 4 3 2 1","1 3 5","5 3 1"],3,"A negative step counts down, and the loop stops before reaching the stop value 0: 5, 3, 1.","Objective 2.2 range()"],["pc21",0,2,"What does this code print?\ni = 0\nwhile i < 3:\n    i += 1\nelse:\n    print(\"done\", i)",["done 2","done 3","Nothing","done 4"],1,"The else clause of a while loop runs when the condition becomes false without a break. The loop stops when i reaches 3, then the else prints done 3.","Objective 2.2 while-else"],["pc22",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor n in range(5):\n    if n == 3:\n        break\nelse:\n    print(\"no break\")\nprint(n)",["no break, then 3","4","no break, then 4","3"],3,"break leaves the loop when n is 3, and a for loop's else clause is skipped when the loop ends with break. n keeps its last value, 3.","Objective 2.2 for-else and break"],["pc23",0,2,"A developer wants the sum of the odd numbers from 1 to 5. What does this code print?\ntotal = 0\nfor n in range(1, 6):\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        continue\n    total += n\nprint(total)",["6","15","9","4"],2,"continue skips the rest of the body for even n, so only 1 + 3 + 5 = 9 is added. 6 would be the even numbers and 15 all of them.","Objective 2.2 continue"],["pc24",0,2,"How many times does the inner loop body run?\nc = 0\nfor i in range(3):\n    for j in range(i):\n        c += 1\nprint(c)",["9","6","3","2"],2,"The inner range(i) runs 0, 1 and 2 times as i goes 0, 1, 2, for a total of 3. 9 assumes the inner loop always runs three times.","Objective 2.2 Nested loops"],["pc25",0,2,"What does this code print?\nn = 10\nwhile True:\n    n -= 3\n    if n < 0:\n        break\nprint(n)",["-2","1","-1","0"],0,"n goes 10, 7, 4, 1, -2. The check happens after subtracting, so the loop breaks only once n is already -2.","Objective 2.2 break"],["pc26",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor i in range(0):\n    print(\"x\")\nelse:\n    print(\"empty\")",["x","Nothing at all","empty","x, then empty"],2,"range(0) is empty, so the body never runs. The loop still ends without break, so the else clause prints empty.","Objective 2.2 for-else"],["pc27",0,2,"A developer writes an if block at the top level of a module (not inside any loop) but has not written its body yet. What should go in the body so the file still runs?",["A comment such as # TODO","pass","continue","break"],1,"pass is a do-nothing statement that satisfies the need for an indented body. A comment alone is not a statement, and continue or break outside a loop are syntax errors.","Objective 2.2 pass"],["pc28",0,2,"A grading script gives every score of 85 a D. What is the bug?\nscore = 85\nif score >= 60:\n    grade = \"D\"\nelif score >= 80:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"F\"\nprint(grade)",["elif needs its own else","85 is compared as a string","The broader condition is tested first, so the elif never runs","The else branch runs first"],2,"Conditions are tested top to bottom and 85 >= 60 matches first, so grade is D and the elif is never reached. The more specific test (>= 80) must come first.","Objective 2.1 Conditional statements"],["pc29",0,2,"What does this code print?\nx = 0\nwhile x < 10:\n    x += 4\nprint(x)",["10","8","12","9"],2,"x goes 4, 8, 12. The condition is checked only at the start of each pass, so x can overshoot 10 before the loop stops.","Objective 2.2 while"],["pc30",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor ch in \"abc\":\n    print(ch * 2, end=\"\")",["abcabc","a a b b c c","abc","aabbcc"],3,"A for loop iterates over a string one character at a time; each character is repeated twice and end='' keeps them on one line.","Objective 2.2 Iterating sequences"],["pc31",0,2,"What does this code print?\na = 5\nb = 10\nif a > 3:\n    if b < 5:\n        print(\"X\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Y\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Z\")",["Y","X","Z","Y, then Z"],0,"a > 3 is true, so the outer if runs. Inside it, b < 5 is false, so the nested else prints Y. The outer else is skipped.","Objective 2.1 Nested conditionals"],["pc32",0,2,"How many lines does this code print?\nfor i in range(1, 10, 4):\n    print(\"hi\")",["2","4","3","9"],2,"range(1, 10, 4) produces 1, 5 and 9, so hi prints three times. 9 is included because it is below the stop value 10.","Objective 2.2 range()"],["pc33",0,2,"What does this code print?\ni = 5\nwhile i > 0:\n    if i == 2:\n        break\n    i -= 1\nelse:\n    print(\"else\")\nprint(i)",["else, then 2","0","else, then 0","2"],3,"i drops from 5 to 2, then break exits the loop. Because the loop ended with break, the while loop's else clause is skipped.","Objective 2.2 while-else and break"],["pc34",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor i in range(3):\n    pass\nprint(i)",["3","2","0","A NameError"],1,"The loop variable keeps its last value after a for loop ends. range(3) ends at 2, so i is 2. pass does nothing.","Objective 2.2 for"],["pc35",0,2,"A student's program prints 0 over and over and never stops. What is the problem?\ncount = 0\nwhile count < 5:\n    print(count)",["print() cannot be used inside a while loop","while needs an else clause to stop","count is never changed inside the loop","The condition must use <= instead of <"],2,"The condition count < 5 stays true forever because nothing in the body changes count. Adding count += 1 fixes it; the comparison operator is not the issue.","Objective 2.2 while"],["pc36",0,2,"A program must keep asking the user for a PIN until a valid one is entered. How many attempts are needed is not known in advance. Which loop fits best?",["A while loop that repeats until the input is valid","A for loop over range(3)","A single if-else statement","A for loop over the PIN string"],0,"while repeats as long as a condition holds, which suits an unknown number of repetitions. A for loop over range(3) caps the attempts at three.","Objective 2.2 while"],["pc37",0,2,"How many times does this code call print()?\nc = 0\nfor i in range(10, 0):\n    c += 1\nprint(c)",["10","1","0","9"],2,"With the default step of +1, range(10, 0) is empty because the start is already past the stop. Counting down needs a negative step.","Objective 2.2 range()"],["pc38",0,2,"How many lines does this nested loop print?\nfor i in range(3):\n    for j in range(3):\n        if j == 1:\n            break\n        print(i, j)",["1","3","9","6"],1,"break exits only the inner loop. For each of the three outer values, the inner loop prints j = 0, then breaks at j = 1: three lines.","Objective 2.2 Nested loops and break"],["pc39",0,2,"What does this code print?\nx = 4\nif x > 2 and x < 4:\n    print(\"in\")\nelif x == 4 or x == 5:\n    print(\"edge\")\nelse:\n    print(\"out\")",["edge","in","out","in, then edge"],0,"x > 2 and x < 4 is False because 4 < 4 is False. The elif checks x == 4 or x == 5, which is True, so edge prints.","Objective 2.1 Conditional statements"],["pc40",0,2,"What does this code print?\ntotal = 0\nfor i in range(0, 11, 2):\n    total += i\nprint(total)",["20","25","30","55"],2,"range(0, 11, 2) produces 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10, which add up to 30. 20 forgets that 10 is included because 11 is the stop value.","Objective 2.2 range()"],["pc41",0,2,"A search loop should print Not found only when it checks every item without finding a match. Which construct does this most directly?",["A for loop followed by pass","A for loop with an else clause","A while True loop with continue","An if statement placed before the loop"],1,"The else clause of a for loop runs only when the loop was not ended by break, so breaking on a match skips the Not found message automatically.","Objective 2.2 for-else"],["pc42",0,2,"What does this code print?\nn = 3\nwhile n:\n    print(n, end=\" \")\n    n -= 1",["3 2 1 0","2 1 0","3 2 1","Nothing, the loop never starts"],2,"A non-zero int is truthy, so the loop runs while n is 3, 2 and 1, and stops when n becomes 0, which is falsy.","Objective 2.2 while"],["pc43",0,2,"What happens when this code is run?\nx = 5\nif x = 5:\n    print(\"yes\")",["It prints yes","It prints nothing","It fails with a SyntaxError","It fails with a NameError"],2,"= is assignment, which is not allowed as an if condition; the comparison operator is ==. The error is caught when the code is parsed, so nothing runs.","Objective 2.1 Conditional statements"],["pc44",0,2,"What does this code print?\ni = 0\nwhile i < 5:\n    i += 1\n    if i == 3:\n        continue\n    print(i, end=\"\")",["1245","12345","12","245"],0,"When i is 3, continue skips the print but the loop keeps going. i is incremented before the check, so 1, 2, 4 and 5 print.","Objective 2.2 continue"],["pc45",0,3,"What does this code print?\nnums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nprint(nums[1:4], nums[-2])",["[2, 3, 4, 5] 4","[2, 3, 4] 4","[1, 2, 3] 3","[2, 3, 4] 5"],1,"A slice includes the start index but not the stop index, so nums[1:4] is [2, 3, 4]. Index -2 is the second item from the end, 4.","Objective 3.1 Lists: slicing"],["pc46",0,3,"A developer copies a list with b = a and then changes b. What does this print?\na = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a\nb.append(4)\nprint(a)",["[1, 2, 3]","[4]","[4, 1, 2, 3]","[1, 2, 3, 4]"],3,"b = a copies the reference, not the list, so both names point to the same list and the append is visible through a. Use a[:] or list(a) for a real copy.","Objective 3.1 Lists: copying"],["pc47",0,3,"What does this code print?\na = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a[:]\nb[0] = 99\nprint(a[0])",["99","1","[99, 2, 3]","An IndexError"],1,"a[:] makes a new list (a shallow copy), so changing b[0] does not affect a. With b = a instead, the answer would be 99.","Objective 3.1 Lists: cloning"],["pc48",0,3,"What does this code print?\nlst = [3, 1, 2]\nlst.insert(1, 9)\nprint(lst)",["[3, 9, 1, 2]","[9, 3, 1, 2]","[3, 1, 9, 2]","[3, 1, 2, 9]"],0,"insert(i, x) puts x before the item currently at index i, so 9 becomes the new item at index 1. append would add it at the end.","Objective 3.1 Lists: methods"],["pc49",0,3,"What does this code print?\nlst = [5, 3, 8]\nprint(sorted(lst), lst)",["[3, 5, 8] [5, 3, 8]","[3, 5, 8] [3, 5, 8]","None [3, 5, 8]","[5, 3, 8] [3, 5, 8]"],0,"sorted() returns a new sorted list and leaves the original unchanged. The list's own sort() method is the one that sorts in place.","Objective 3.1 Lists: sorted()"],["pc50",0,3,"What does this code print?\nlst = [10, 20, 30, 40]\ndel lst[1:3]\nprint(lst)",["[10, 40]","[10, 30, 40]","[40]","[10, 20, 40]"],0,"del with a slice removes indexes 1 and 2 (20 and 30); the stop index 3 is not included.","Objective 3.1 Lists: del"],["pc51",0,3,"What does this list comprehension print?\nprint([x * x for x in range(5) if x % 2])",["[0, 4, 16]","[1, 3]","[1, 9, 25]","[1, 9]"],3,"x % 2 is truthy only for odd x, so the filter keeps 1 and 3 from range(5) and squares them. 25 would need x = 5, which range(5) never produces.","Objective 3.1 List comprehensions"],["pc52",0,3,"A program stores a 2 x 3 matrix as nested lists. What does this print?\nm = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nprint(m[1][0] + m[0][2])",["5","6","7","9"],2,"m[1][0] is row 1, column 0, which is 4, and m[0][2] is row 0, column 2, which is 3. 4 + 3 = 7.","Objective 3.1 Nested lists"],["pc53",0,3,"What happens when this code runs?\nt = (1, 2, 3)\nt[0] = 9",["A TypeError is raised","t becomes (9, 2, 3)","An IndexError is raised","The assignment is silently ignored"],0,"Tuples are immutable, so assigning to an item raises TypeError. The index 0 is valid, so it is not an IndexError.","Objective 3.2 Tuples: immutability"],["pc54",0,3,"What does this code print?\nt = (5)\nu = (5,)\nprint(t * 2, u * 2)",["(5, 5) (5, 5)","10 10","(10,) (5, 5)","10 (5, 5)"],3,"Parentheses alone do not make a tuple: (5) is just the int 5, so t * 2 is 10. The trailing comma in (5,) makes a one-item tuple, and * repeats it.","Objective 3.2 Tuples: building"],["pc55",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\nd[\"c\"] = 3\nd[\"a\"] = 10\nprint(len(d), d[\"a\"])",["3 1","4 10","3 10","2 10"],2,"Assigning to a new key adds it, and assigning to an existing key replaces its value. The dictionary ends with keys a, b and c, and a is 10.","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries"],["pc56",0,3,"A script reads a setting that is missing from a dictionary. What happens?\nd = {\"x\": 1}\nprint(d[\"y\"])",["A KeyError is raised","None is printed","An IndexError is raised","An empty string is printed"],0,"Square-bracket lookup of a missing key raises KeyError. IndexError is for sequences such as lists; dictionaries do not return None for missing keys with [].","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries"],["pc57",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\nfor k, v in d.items():\n    print(k, v, end=\";\")",["a;b;","1 a;2 b;","a 1;b 2;","('a', 1);('b', 2);"],2,"items() yields key-value pairs, which the loop unpacks into k and v. Dictionaries keep insertion order, so a comes first.","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries: items()"],["pc58",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"a\": 1}\nprint(1 in d, \"a\" in d)",["True True","True False","False False","False True"],3,"The in operator checks a dictionary's keys, not its values. 1 is a value, so 1 in d is False, while 'a' is a key.","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries: key existence"],["pc59",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\ndel d[\"a\"]\nprint(d)",["{'b': 2}","{'a': 1}","{'b': 2, 'a': None}","{}"],0,"del d[key] removes that key and its value from the dictionary, leaving only b.","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries: removing"],["pc60",0,3,"A developer tries to capitalize the first letter of a string in place. What happens?\ns = \"hello\"\ns[0] = \"H\"",["s becomes Hello","s becomes H","An IndexError is raised","A TypeError is raised"],3,"Strings are immutable, so item assignment raises TypeError. A new string must be built instead, for example 'H' + s[1:].","Objective 3.4 Strings: immutability"],["pc61",0,3,"What does this code print?\ns = \"Python\"\nprint(s[::-1], s[1:3])",["nohtyP yth","nohtyP yt","Python yt","nohtyP Py"],1,"A step of -1 walks the string backward, reversing it. s[1:3] takes indexes 1 and 2 (y and t); the stop index is excluded.","Objective 3.4 Strings: slicing"],["pc62",0,3,"A CSV line has an empty field. What does this code print?\nprint(\"a,b,,c\".split(\",\"))",["['a', 'b', '', 'c']","['a', 'b', 'c']","['a,b,,c']","['a', 'b', ',', 'c']"],0,"split(',') cuts at every comma, so two commas in a row produce an empty string between them. Empty fields are not dropped when a separator is given.","Objective 3.4 Strings: methods"],["pc63",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(\"-\".join([\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"]))",["-x-y-z-","x-y-z","xyz","['x', 'y', 'z']"],1,"sep.join(list) puts the separator only between items and returns one string. No separator is added at the start or end.","Objective 3.4 Strings: methods"],["pc64",0,3,"What does this code print?\ns = 'It\\'s'\nprint(len(s))",["5","4","6","A SyntaxError"],1,"\\' is an escaped apostrophe that counts as one character, so the string is It's with length 4. The backslash itself is not stored.","Objective 3.4 Strings: escaping"],["pc65",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(\"abc\" < \"abd\", \"Z\" < \"a\")",["True False","False True","False False","True True"],3,"Strings compare character by character by code point. 'abc' < 'abd' because c < d, and uppercase letters have lower code points than lowercase, so 'Z' < 'a'.","Objective 3.4 Strings: comparison"],["pc66",0,3,"What does this code print?\ns = \"\"\"a\nb\"\"\"\nprint(len(s))",["2","4","3","1"],2,"A triple-quoted string keeps the line break as a real newline character, so the string holds a, a newline and b: three characters.","Objective 3.4 Strings: multiline"],["pc67",0,3,"What does this code print?\nlst = [1, 2, 3, 2]\nlst.remove(2)\nprint(lst, lst.index(2))",["[1, 3] 1","[1, 3, 2] 2","[1, 2, 3] 1","[1, 3, 2] 1"],1,"remove() deletes only the first matching value, leaving [1, 3, 2]. index(2) then finds 2 at position 2.","Objective 3.1 Lists: methods"],["pc68",0,3,"A program must store the fixed RGB values of a company color so other code cannot change them by accident. Which type fits best?",["A list such as [0, 102, 204]","A tuple such as (0, 102, 204)","A string such as '0,102,204'","A dictionary with index keys"],1,"Tuples are immutable, so their items cannot be reassigned, which protects fixed data. A list works the same way for reading but can be changed.","Objective 3.2 Tuples vs lists"],["pc69",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(3 in [1, 2, 3], \"x\" not in \"xyz\")",["True True","False False","False True","True False"],3,"3 is in the list, so the first test is True. in on a string checks for a substring; x is in xyz, so not in gives False.","Objective 3.1 Lists: in and not in"],["pc70",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(a, b=2):\n    return a * b\nprint(f(3), f(3, 4))",["6 6","3 12","A TypeError","6 12"],3,"f(3) uses the default b = 2, giving 6. f(3, 4) overrides the default, giving 12. A default makes the argument optional, not fixed.","Objective 4.2 Default parameters"],["pc71",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f():\n    print(\"hi\")\nx = f()\nprint(x)",["hi, then None","hi","None","hi, then hi"],0,"f() prints hi when called and has no return statement, so it returns None. That None is stored in x and printed.","Objective 4.1 None and return"],["pc72",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(a, b, c):\n    return a - b + c\nprint(f(1, c=3, b=2))",["0","2","4","A TypeError"],1,"Keyword arguments match by name, so a = 1, b = 2 and c = 3, giving 1 - 2 + 3 = 2. Keyword arguments can come in any order after positional ones.","Objective 4.2 Keyword arguments"],["pc73",0,4,"What happens when this code runs?\ndef f(a, b):\n    return a + b\nprint(f(a=1, 2))",["It prints 3","It prints -1","A NameError is raised","A SyntaxError is raised"],3,"A positional argument cannot follow a keyword argument in a call. Python rejects the code when parsing, before the function ever runs.","Objective 4.2 Mixing argument types"],["pc74",0,4,"A developer writes this function header. What happens?\ndef f(a=1, b):\n    return a + b",["It works, and b defaults to None","A TypeError when the function is called","A SyntaxError, because a default parameter comes before a required one","It works, but only with keyword arguments"],2,"Parameters with default values must come after all parameters without defaults. The definition itself fails with SyntaxError.","Objective 4.2 Default parameters"],["pc75",0,4,"What does this code print?\nx = 10\ndef f():\n    x = 5\n    return x\nprint(f(), x)",["5 5","10 10","10 5","5 10"],3,"Assigning x inside f creates a local variable that shadows the global one. The global x stays 10.","Objective 4.3 Scopes and shadowing"],["pc76",0,4,"What does this code print?\nx = 1\ndef f():\n    global x\n    x += 1\nf()\nprint(x)",["2","1","A NameError","An UnboundLocalError"],0,"The global keyword makes x inside f refer to the module-level x, so x += 1 changes it to 2. Without global the code would raise an error.","Objective 4.3 global keyword"],["pc77",0,4,"What happens when this code runs?\nx = 1\ndef f():\n    x += 1\n    print(x)\nf()",["It prints 2","It prints 1","An UnboundLocalError is raised","A SyntaxError is raised"],2,"Because x is assigned inside f, Python treats x as local for the whole function. x += 1 then reads the local x before it has a value. Declaring global x would fix it.","Objective 4.3 Scopes"],["pc78",0,4,"What does this recursive function print?\ndef f(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    return n * f(n - 1)\nprint(f(4))",["10","24","12","4"],1,"f(4) = 4 * f(3) = 4 * 3 * f(2) = 4 * 3 * 2 * f(1), and the base case returns 1, so the result is 24 (4 factorial).","Objective 4.1 Recursion"],["pc79",0,4,"A recursive function has no base case, so it always calls itself again. What happens when it runs?\ndef f(n):\n    return f(n + 1)\nf(0)",["It runs forever with no error","It returns None after 10 calls","Python stops it with a RecursionError","A SyntaxError is raised when it is defined"],2,"Python limits recursion depth, so endless recursion raises RecursionError. The definition is valid syntax, so nothing fails until it runs.","Objective 4.1 Recursion"],["pc80",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef add(lst):\n    lst.append(1)\nnums = []\nadd(nums)\nadd(nums)\nprint(nums)",["[]","[1]","[1, 1]","None"],2,"A list is passed by reference to the same object, so append inside the function changes the caller's list. Two calls add two items.","Objective 4.2 Passing arguments"],["pc81",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f():\n    return 1, 2\na, b = f()\nprint(b, a)",["1 2","2 1","(1, 2)","A ValueError"],1,"return 1, 2 returns a tuple, which is unpacked into a and b. The print then shows b first.","Objective 4.1 Returning results"],["pc82",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef gen():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\nfor v in gen():\n    print(v, end=\" \")",["1","1 2","2","None"],1,"A function containing yield is a generator. Each yield hands one value to the for loop and resumes afterwards, so both 1 and 2 are printed.","Objective 4.1 yield"],["pc83",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntry:\n    print(1 / 0)\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(\"zero\")\nexcept ArithmeticError:\n    print(\"arith\")",["arith","zero, then arith","Nothing, the program crashes","zero"],3,"Python runs the first matching except branch only. ZeroDivisionError matches first, so arith is never printed.","Objective 4.5 Ordering except branches"],["pc84",0,4,"A developer puts the general branch first. What does this code print?\ntry:\n    print(1 / 0)\nexcept ArithmeticError:\n    print(\"arith\")\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(\"zero\")",["zero","arith","zero, then arith","Nothing, the program crashes"],1,"ZeroDivisionError is a subclass of ArithmeticError, so the first branch already matches and the more specific branch is never reached. Specific exceptions should come first.","Objective 4.5 Ordering except branches"],["pc85",0,4,"A program converts user input to a number. What does this code print?\ntry:\n    int(\"abc\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"V\")\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"T\")",["T","V","V, then T","Nothing, the program crashes"],1,"int('abc') has the right type (a string) but a value that cannot be converted, so it raises ValueError. TypeError is for the wrong type, such as int([1]).","Objective 4.4 Built-in exceptions"],["pc86",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntry:\n    x = [1, 2][5]\nexcept LookupError:\n    print(\"L\")\nexcept IndexError:\n    print(\"I\")",["I","L, then I","Nothing, the program crashes","L"],3,"An out-of-range index raises IndexError, which is a subclass of LookupError, so the first branch catches it.","Objective 4.4 Exception hierarchy"],["pc87",0,4,"A program uses except Exception: to catch all errors. Which exception will that branch not catch?",["ValueError","KeyboardInterrupt","KeyError","ZeroDivisionError"],1,"KeyboardInterrupt (like SystemExit) inherits directly from BaseException, not from Exception, so the user can still stop the program. The other three are all Exception subclasses.","Objective 4.4 Exception hierarchy"],["pc88",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f():\n    return 1 / 0\ndef g():\n    try:\n        f()\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        print(\"caught in g\")\ng()",["Nothing, the program crashes","caught in g, then None","caught in g","None"],2,"The exception raised in f is not handled there, so it propagates up to the caller g, whose except branch handles it.","Objective 4.5 Propagating exceptions"],["pc89",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntry:\n    print(\"a\" + 1)\nexcept:\n    print(\"E\")",["a1","E","Nothing, the program crashes","E, then a1"],1,"Adding a string and an int raises TypeError. A bare except catches any exception, so E is printed. It is usually better to name the exception.","Objective 4.5 try-except"],["pc90",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(x):\n    if x > 0:\n        return \"pos\"\nprint(f(-1))",["None","pos","0","An error, because there is no return value"],0,"For x = -1 the if body never runs, so the function reaches its end without return and returns None, which print shows.","Objective 4.1 None"],["pc91",0,4,"A function is defined as def area(w, h): and called as area(3, 4). What are 3 and 4?",["Parameters","Arguments","Keywords","Literals of the function"],1,"Parameters are the names in the definition (w and h); arguments are the values passed in the call. They are easy to mix up.","Objective 4.2 Parameters vs arguments"],["pc92",0,4,"What happens when this code runs?\nprint(sq(3))\ndef sq(n):\n    return n * n",["A NameError is raised","It prints 9","A SyntaxError is raised","It prints None"],0,"Python runs top to bottom, so sq does not exist yet when it is called. Define a function before the line that calls it.","Objective 4.1 Defining and invoking functions"],["pc93",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntry:\n    {}[\"k\"]\nexcept (KeyError, IndexError):\n    print(\"lookup\")",["lookup","Nothing, the program crashes","{}","k"],0,"One except branch can name several exceptions in a tuple. Looking up a missing key raises KeyError, which the tuple matches.","Objective 4.5 try-except"],["pc94",0,4,"A script calls exit() to stop early. Which exception does this raise?",["SystemExit","KeyboardInterrupt","StopIteration","RuntimeError"],0,"exit() and sys.exit() raise SystemExit, which inherits from BaseException. KeyboardInterrupt is raised when the user presses Ctrl+C.","Objective 4.4 Exception hierarchy"],["pc95",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(n, s=\"*\"):\n    return s * n\nprint(f(3), f(2, \"ab\"))",["*** ***","3* 2ab","A TypeError","*** abab"],3,"f(3) uses the default s = '*' and repeats it 3 times. f(2, 'ab') overrides the default, giving abab.","Objective 4.2 Default parameters"],["pc96",0,4,"What does this code print?\nx = 3\ndef f():\n    return x * 2\nx = 5\nprint(f())",["10","6","A NameError","8"],0,"f only reads x, so it uses the global x as it is when f is called, not when f is defined. x is 5 by then, so f returns 10.","Objective 4.3 Scopes"],["pc97",0,4,"A team splits a 300-line script into small functions such as read_scores() and print_report(). What is the main benefit?",["Code is easier to test, reuse and read","The script always runs faster","Variables become global automatically","Exceptions can no longer occur"],0,"Decomposition breaks a problem into named, reusable pieces that can be tested one at a time. It does not make code faster or stop errors.","Objective 4.1 Decomposition"]]);
